          "Նաիրի is an old name. It appears in Urartian and Assyrian inscriptions for a confederation of peoples and lands around Lake Van, centuries before the word \"Armenia\" existed, and by the nineteenth century Armenian writers had adopted it as a poetic, elevated synonym for the Armenian homeland — used the way English poetry might reach for \"Albion\" rather than \"England.\" Charents's title uses that inherited grandeur on purpose, and then spends the book testing it against a much smaller and shabbier reality.",
